1:1-1 (* EXPECT-ERROR: an allocation in pool scratch escapes its pool *)
2:1-15 (* The same slice leaks through a field.  It was observed 2026-10-03\n   writing Png.Raster.  A canvas record held its pixels in a field, the\n   pixels were allocated in the procedure's own scratch pool, and the\n   procedure answered `c.pix'.  `RETURN c', the whole record, was\n   refused: a frame value stored into a component had tainted the local\n   since stage 5 of the pool elision.  `RETURN c.pix' was not refused,\n   because the storage rules followed a bare local and a view of one,\n   never a component.  The checker said nothing, the C compiled, and the\n   caller read freed memory in Png.Encode.\n\n   A component that was itself given frame storage now carries it, by\n   its path: `c.pix', not `c'.  So an element of a slice that merely\n   LIVES in a local pool (a string header whose bytes were made\n   elsewhere) is still a value anyone may keep. *)
29:38-29 (* dies with scratch, on the way out *)
comments=3
